@inject ApiMetadata Api
@* Card's Overview, written by hand after Button's and Form's: the header and variants, sizes, a
   linked card with media, a heading of your own, the body and footer, flush content, sections, a
   form in a card (each mode posts it differently) and a grid of cards. Accessibility, Parts and
   See also come from the doc comment and components.json. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A card is a framed box around one thing the user looks at or acts on, such as a project, a person or a plan. It suits a grid of things like it. For a band across the page, such as a hero, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/section">Section</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Variants</Heading>
<TextBlock>The variant sets how the card stands apart from the page: <Code>Outline</Code> (the default) has a border, <Code>Elevated</Code> a shadow, and <Code>Filled</Code> a tinted background. Set <Code>Variant</Code> to choose one. To give the card a header, set <Code>Title</Code> and <Code>Description</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-variants" />
<TextBlock>The title is an <Code>h3</Code>. To fit the page's outline, set <Code>TitleLevel</Code>. Content between the tags is the body.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes</Heading>
<TextBlock>The size sets the padding and the title's size. <Code>Sm</Code> suits dense grids, <Code>Md</Code> is the default, and <Code>Lg</Code> suits a card on its own. Set <Code>Size</Code> to choose one.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-sizes" />

<Heading Level="2">A linked card</Heading>
<TextBlock>A linked card opens a page when the user clicks anywhere on it. Set <Code>Href</Code>; the link is the card's title, so a linked card needs a <Code>Title</Code>. Buttons in <Code>CardActions</Code>, <Code>CardFooter</Code> and the body stay clickable on their own.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-linked" />
<List>
    <ListItem>A <Code>CardMedia</Code> shows an image, video or SVG across the top, edge to edge.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Media that only decorates needs <Code>alt=""</Code> on an <Code>img</Code>, or <Code>aria-hidden="true"</Code> on an <Code>svg</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>On a linked card, an image is part of the link, and a video's controls stay usable above it.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Header content</Heading>
<TextBlock>The card's heading row can contain more than the title: content above it, such as who wrote the post, and buttons at its end. Put content above the title in a <Code>CardHeader</Code>. Put buttons or a <Anchor Href="docs/components/menu">Menu</Anchor> at the end of the heading row in a <Code>CardActions</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-post" />

<Heading Level="2">Body and footer</Heading>
<TextBlock>The body is the card's content, and the footer is a row of buttons at the bottom. To lay out the body, put it in a <Code>CardBody</Code> and set <Code>Gap</Code>, <Code>Align</Code> and <Code>Direction</Code>. For the footer, add a <Code>CardFooter</Code> and set <Code>Justify</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-footer" />
<TextBlock>Write the footer last, directly in the card. Inside a <Code>CardBody</Code> or a form, it's an ordinary row, not the card's footer.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Flush content</Heading>
<TextBlock>Flush content runs to the card's edges, with no padding, such as a <Anchor Href="docs/components/table">Table</Anchor> or a divided list. Set <Code>Flush</Code> on a <Code>CardBody</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-flush" />

<Heading Level="2">Sections and a footer bar</Heading>
<TextBlock>A card can be split into sections with a line between each. Set <Code>Dividers</Code> on the card, and each <Code>CardBody</Code> becomes a section. To make the footer a bar across the bottom, set <Code>Bg</Code> or <Code>BorderTop</Code> on the <Code>CardFooter</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-sections" />

<Heading Level="2">A form in a card</Heading>
<TextBlock>A card can frame a form, with its submit button in the card's footer. Put the <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor> around the card, so the submit button in its <Code>CardFooter</Code> is inside the form.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-settings" />
<TextBlock>A form inside the card would reach none of the card's own parts. A <Code>CardFooter</Code> inside a form is an ordinary row, and <Code>CardMedia</Code>, <Code>CardHeader</Code> and <Code>CardActions</Code> belong to the card itself. A form whose buttons are in its own <Code>FormActions</Code> can go inside.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Cards in a grid</Heading>
<TextBlock>A grid of cards fits as many cards to a row as there's room for. Put the cards in a <Anchor Href="docs/components/grid">Grid</Anchor> and set <Code>MinChildWidth</Code>. The cards in a row share its height, and each <Code>CardFooter</Code> stays at the bottom.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="card-projects" />
<TextBlock>Put the body's text in an element, such as a <Code>TextBlock</Code>. Above a footer, bare text gets the body's gap, not a section's space.</TextBlock>

@if (OverviewParts.Of(Component, Api).Count > 0)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Parts</Heading>
}
<OverviewParts Component="Component" />

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Card Title="Website" Description="Updated 2 hours ago">
            <TextBlock>Marketing pages and the docs, deployed from main.</TextBlock>
            <CardFooter Justify="Justify.End">
                <Button Href="projects/website" Size="ControlSize.Sm">Open</Button>
            </CardFooter>
        </Card>

        @* The whole card is a link, through its title. *@
        <Card Title="Course app" Description="12 lessons" Href="courses/app" />
        """;
}
